WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.400
SAST helps teams identify risky code patterns early, before software reaches production.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:18.340
It analyzes source code, bytecode, or artifacts without requiring a live attack scenario, and it can provide feedback during development, pull request review, CI/CD, release preparation, and ongoing maintenance.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.880
Its value is speed and consistency, but its output still needs context.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:34.300
Strong SAST programs combine accurate configuration, useful rules, developer-friendly workflows, triage discipline, practical remediation, pipeline integration, and governance.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.640
Findings are potential issues, not automatic proof.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:46.020
Reviewers must consider reachability, exposure, data sensitivity, confidence, false positives, false negatives, and compensating controls.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:49.760
Prioritization turns tool output into risk-based action.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:58.460
SAST works best when paired with secure design, manual code review, dependency scanning, runtime testing, monitoring, and vulnerability response.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:05.880
Static analysis can reason about code paths before execution, but it cannot fully understand every business rule or runtime condition.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:11.940
Other practices fill those gaps and help validate whether software behaves securely in the real environment.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:14.560
The goal is not to generate more alerts.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:19.420
The goal is actionable evidence that helps developers reduce software risk over time.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:30.200
A durable SAST program teaches secure patterns, validates fixes, controls exceptions, measures improvement, and uses recurring findings to strengthen the development lifecycle.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:35.700
Done well, SAST becomes a quiet source of engineering quality rather than a noisy final gate.
